A = {
180: """These days, people are talking a lot about new trends in take-out and delivery food. The biggest change is that almost everyone orders through apps now. With just a few taps, you can choose from hundreds of restaurants, see photos and reviews, and track your order in real time. The apps also offer lots of discount codes and free delivery, which makes ordering very attractive. Another trend is that restaurants are creating special menus just for delivery, like combo meals for one person or family sets that are easy to share. There are even "cloud kitchens," which only cook for delivery and don't have any seats for customers. Also, more restaurants are offering healthy options and smaller portions. Some people are concerned about the amount of plastic packaging, so a few apps now let you choose not to receive plastic spoons and forks, which I think is a good idea.""",
181: """In my country, busy working people usually get their meals in a few different ways on weekdays. For breakfast, most people grab something quick on the way to work, like a banh mi or a bowl of pho from a street stall, and they often eat it in just ten minutes. For lunch, many people who work in factories or big companies, like me, eat at the company canteen, because it's cheap and convenient. Office workers in the city often go out to small restaurants near their office with their coworkers, or they order food through delivery apps and eat at their desks. Some people also bring lunch boxes from home to save money and eat healthier. For dinner, most families still try to cook and eat together at home. But on busy days, more and more people order food delivery instead of cooking.""",
182: """When I was a child, gatherings and celebrations were mostly family events held at home. For example, on Tet or at death anniversaries, all our relatives came to my grandparents' house. The women spent the whole day cooking in the kitchen, the men drank and chatted, and the kids ran around the yard. Everything was homemade, and it was very warm, but also a lot of work. These days, things are quite different. Many families now celebrate at restaurants instead of cooking at home, because people are busier and the houses in the city are smaller. Celebrations have also become more varied. More recently, birthday parties for kids have become very popular, often with themes and decorations. Young people also celebrate things like Christmas, Valentine's Day, and Halloween, which weren't common before. Another popular trend is company year-end parties, which are often big events with games and performances.""",
183: """Gatherings and celebrations are important in the city, but organizing them is not easy, and people often talk about several issues. The first one is cost. Things like renting a restaurant, food, decorations, and gifts can be really expensive, especially for big events like weddings. The second issue is time. People in the city are very busy with work, so finding a date when everyone can come is difficult, and planning takes a lot of energy. Another issue is space. Most people live in apartments, so they can't host big parties at home and have to book restaurants or event halls in advance, especially during the wedding season. Some people also talk about the pressure of social expectations, like feeling they have to invite many guests or give expensive gifts. Because of these issues, many people now prefer smaller and simpler celebrations with close family and friends.""",
184: """Celebrations in small towns and big cities in my country have some similarities and differences. In terms of similarities, family is the center of celebrations everywhere. Whether it's Tet, a wedding, or a birthday, people always get together, eat a lot of food, and share good wishes. However, there are several clear differences. In small towns, celebrations are usually held at home or in a tent in front of the house, and the whole neighborhood often joins in. Neighbors help each other cook and prepare, so it feels like a community event. In big cities, celebrations are more often held at restaurants, hotels, or event halls, and they're usually more organized and professional. City celebrations also tend to be smaller, with only close friends and family. Personally, I think small-town celebrations are warmer, but city celebrations are more convenient and less tiring for the hosts.""",
185: """In my area, people often complain about a few problems related to gatherings and celebrations. The most common one is noise. Some families hold parties or weddings with loud karaoke that goes on until late at night, which bothers neighbors, especially people with small children. Another big issue is traffic and parking. When there's a big wedding or celebration, guests park their motorbikes and cars all over the street, which causes traffic jams. Sometimes families even set up tents that block the whole road. Garbage is also a problem. After big events, there's often a lot of trash, like plastic cups and food, left on the street. Some people also worry about drinking too much, which can lead to arguments or traffic accidents when guests drive home. I think people should be more considerate of their neighbors, and local authorities should have clearer rules.""",
186: """Food shopping has changed a lot over the last twenty years in my country. In the past, most people bought their food at traditional wet markets every day. They bought fresh vegetables, meat, and fish in the morning, and they knew the sellers personally. There were very few supermarkets, and they were mostly in big cities. These days, things are very different. Supermarkets and convenience stores are everywhere, and many people prefer them because they're clean, air-conditioned, and the prices are clearly marked. Another big change is online shopping. Many people now order groceries through apps and get them delivered within a couple of hours, which is very convenient for busy families. People also care more about food safety, so organic stores and stores that show where the food comes from have become popular. However, many older people still prefer traditional markets because the food is very fresh and they can bargain.""",
187: """I remember a news report about a food poisoning incident a couple of years ago. It happened in a province in the south of Vietnam. According to the news, hundreds of people got sick after eating banh mi, which is a Vietnamese sandwich, from the same shop. Many of them had stomachaches, fevers, and vomiting, and a lot of people had to go to the hospital, including children. The authorities closed the shop and tested the food. Later, they found that some of the ingredients, like the pate and the meat, were contaminated with dangerous bacteria, probably because they weren't stored properly in the hot weather. This news made a lot of people worried about street food safety. Afterwards, many people became more careful about where they buy food, and the local government started checking food stalls more often. I still eat street food, but I choose clean places.""",
188: """Different generations in my country definitely have different views on health. My parents' generation grew up at a time when food was not always easy to get. So for them, being a bit chubby was a sign of good health. If someone looked thin, people would worry that they were sick or not eating enough. My mother still tells me I look too thin every time she sees me. They also didn't think much about exercise, because their daily work was already very physical. My generation sees health differently. Most young people believe that being healthy means being fit and strong, not just skinny or fat. Many people go to the gym, track their diet, and pay attention to things like sugar and calories. We also care more about mental health and sleep. However, I think we can learn from our parents, too, because they cooked at home more and lived a simpler life.""",
189: """A recent news story about health that I remember was about a dengue fever outbreak in my city last year. Dengue is a disease spread by mosquitoes, and it can be very dangerous, especially for children. According to the news, the number of cases was much higher than usual because of the long rainy season, and many hospitals were overloaded with patients. Some people even had to share beds. The news explained that mosquitoes breed in standing water, like in flower pots, old tires, or water containers around houses. My community reacted quickly. The local government organized campaigns to spray chemicals to kill mosquitoes in every neighborhood. People also cleaned their houses and threw away containers that collected rainwater. In my building, the management office sent messages reminding everyone to check for standing water. I became more careful too, and I made sure my daughter always slept under a mosquito net.""",
190: """A few years ago, I used to go to a small gym near my old rented room. It was in the basement of an old building, so it was quite dark and didn't have air conditioning, just a few big fans. The facilities were very basic. There were some old treadmills, a few benches, and lots of heavy weights, and it was always full of young guys lifting weights. There were no classes and no personal trainers, so everyone just did their own workouts. But it was very cheap, so I didn't mind. The gym I go to now is very different. It's in a modern building, and it's bright, clean, and air-conditioned. It has new machines with screens, a separate area for yoga and group classes, and even a sauna. There are also personal trainers who can help you plan your workouts. Of course, it's more expensive, but it's much more comfortable.""",
191: """My country has changed a lot in the past decade, but I'd like to talk about the changes in transportation and infrastructure. Ten years ago, traveling between cities in Vietnam took a long time because the roads were narrow and there were very few highways. For example, driving from Hanoi to the coast could take five or six hours. Today, there are many new highways connecting big cities, so the same trip takes much less time. In big cities, there have also been big changes. Hanoi and Ho Chi Minh City now have metro lines, which is something people were waiting for for many years. There are also many new bridges, overpasses, and airports. Because of these changes, it's much easier for people to travel and for businesses to transport goods. Of course, traffic jams are still a problem in the cities, but overall, the improvement is very clear.""",
192: """I think a country that's geographically similar to Vietnam is Thailand. Both countries are in Southeast Asia and have a tropical climate, with a hot season and a rainy season. Both have mountains in the north, large flat areas with rice fields, and long coastlines with beautiful beaches and islands. Rice farming is also very important in both countries, and they're among the biggest rice exporters in the world. In recent years, Thailand has gone through several changes. Its tourism industry has grown a lot, and it's one of the most visited countries in Asia, with millions of tourists going to Bangkok, Phuket, and Chiang Mai every year. Thailand has also been developing its infrastructure, like new train lines and modern airports. At the same time, like many countries, it has faced challenges, such as an aging population and air pollution in some cities during certain seasons.""",
193: """I recently read an article about Thailand's tourism and its effect on the economy. The article explained that tourism is one of the most important parts of Thailand's economy, and it creates millions of jobs, from hotels and restaurants to tour guides and taxi drivers. It said that after a difficult period when international travel stopped during the pandemic, the number of tourists had recovered strongly, especially visitors from other Asian countries. The government introduced some policies to attract more tourists, like making visas easier for some countries. However, the article also mentioned some challenges, such as overcrowding at popular destinations and the pressure on the environment, like more trash on the beaches. So it was mainly related to the economy, not politics. I found it interesting, because Vietnam is trying to develop its tourism industry too, and we could learn a lot from Thailand's experience.""",
194: """One change I've noticed is in the relationship between Vietnam and South Korea, especially in the economy and culture. Economically, many Korean companies have invested in Vietnam over the past ten years, especially in electronics and manufacturing. They have built huge factories, and they employ hundreds of thousands of Vietnamese workers. In the area where I live, there are many Korean companies, and you can see Korean restaurants and shops everywhere. Culturally, the connection has also become much stronger. Korean dramas, K-pop, and Korean food are extremely popular among young Vietnamese people, and many people are learning Korean to find better jobs. In sports, the relationship became famous when a Korean coach led the Vietnamese football team to some great achievements, which made many Vietnamese people love him. Now, there are also many marriages and friendships between people from the two countries.""",
195: """A historic event that affected the relationship between Vietnam and a neighboring country is when Vietnam and Laos signed a treaty of friendship and cooperation in the late 1970s. Vietnam and Laos share a long border, and the two countries have had a very close relationship for a long time. After the war, the leaders of both countries met and signed this treaty, which promised that the two countries would support each other in areas like the economy, education, and defense. Since then, Vietnam and Laos have often called their relationship a special friendship. Because of this, many Lao students come to study at Vietnamese universities, and Vietnam has helped build roads, schools, and hospitals in Laos. There's also a lot of trade between the two countries. I learned about this event in my history class in high school, and I think it's a good example of friendly cooperation between neighbors.""",
196: """In my country, recycling is becoming more common, but it's still developing. In big cities, people are encouraged to sort their household waste into different types, like recyclable waste, food waste, and other waste. Some apartment buildings, including mine, have separate bins for plastic, paper, and general trash. But the most common way of recycling in Vietnam is through scrap collectors. They go around neighborhoods on bicycles or motorbikes and buy things like old newspapers, cardboard boxes, plastic bottles, and metal cans. People usually save these things at home and sell them for a small amount of money. The collectors then sell them to recycling centers, where they're cleaned, melted down, and made into new products. Some schools and companies also organize recycling campaigns, where people bring old items in exchange for small gifts, like plants or notebooks.""",
197: """At home, my family tries to recycle as much as we can. The most common things we recycle are plastic bottles and cans, especially from drinks and cooking oil. We wash them and keep them in a big bag on the balcony. We also recycle paper and cardboard, like old newspapers, my daughter's old drawing books, and the boxes from online shopping, which we get a lot of. When the bags are full, we sell them to a scrap collector who comes to our neighborhood every week, or we put them in the recycling bin in our building. Besides that, we reuse some things. For example, we use glass jars to store spices and dry food, and we use old T-shirts as cleaning cloths. We also try to reduce plastic bags by bringing our own reusable bags when we go to the supermarket. It's a small effort, but I think it helps.""",
198: """I remember something memorable related to recycling that happened last year. In my apartment building, there's a big recycling area in the basement with separate containers for plastic, paper, and metal. One weekend, after a big online sale, almost every family in the building had a lot of cardboard boxes, and the containers filled up very quickly. On Sunday morning, I went down with a huge pile of boxes, and I saw that the paper container was completely full and boxes were all over the floor. Worse, the collection truck didn't come that Monday as planned because of a holiday. By Tuesday, the area was a big mess, and it even smelled bad because some people threw food waste in the wrong containers. Some residents complained in the building's group chat. In the end, the management office arranged an extra pickup and put up signs to remind people to flatten their boxes.""",
199: """When I was a child, recycling was quite different from now. There weren't any special recycling bins or containers in my village. Instead, people saved recyclable things at home. My mother kept old newspapers, plastic bottles, scrap metal, and even old shoes in a corner of the kitchen. Every few weeks, a woman came to our village with a bicycle and two big baskets, shouting to let people know she was buying scrap. My mother sold everything to her for a little money, and sometimes she let me keep the money to buy candy, which I loved. People also reused a lot of things back then, because they didn't have much money. For example, we used old cans as pencil holders, and my grandmother made bags from old rice sacks. So even though we didn't call it recycling, people were actually very good at not wasting things.""",
200: """The way recycling materials are collected has changed a lot over the years. In the past, there was no official recycling system in my country. Recycling was done mainly by individual scrap collectors who went from house to house on bicycles to buy old newspapers, bottles, and metal. They then sold these items to small recycling workshops, which often weren't very clean or safe. These days, things are becoming more organized. In big cities, there are now rules that require households to sort their waste into different categories, and many apartment buildings have separate bins for recyclables. Some companies have also started recycling programs, and there are more modern recycling factories. In addition, there are apps where you can book someone to pick up your recyclables. However, scrap collectors still play a big role, and many people still sell their recyclables to them. I think the system is improving, but slowly.""",
201: """I remember a news story about plastic waste in the ocean that I watched on TV last year. The story showed some beautiful beaches in central Vietnam that were covered with plastic bottles, bags, and fishing nets after a big storm. It explained that a lot of this plastic came from cities and rivers, and some of it had even floated from other countries. The report said that plastic waste was harming sea turtles, fish, and birds, and it could also affect tourism, which is very important for the local economy. The reaction to the story was quite positive. After it was broadcast, many young volunteers organized beach clean-up events on the weekends, and some hotels and restaurants in the area stopped using plastic straws and bags. People also shared the story on social media to raise awareness. It made me think more about how much plastic my own family uses.""",
202: """People nowadays have several concerns about using the internet. The biggest one is online scams. In Vietnam, many people receive fake messages or calls pretending to be from banks or delivery companies, asking them to click on a link or give their password. Some people have lost a lot of money because of this. Another concern is privacy. Many apps collect personal information, like phone numbers and locations, and sometimes this information is leaked, which leads to lots of spam calls. People are also worried about children, because kids can easily see harmful content or spend too much time online. These concerns have changed people's lives in some ways. Many people are now more careful. They don't click on strange links, they use two-step verification for their bank apps, and parents set time limits on their children's devices. Some older people have even stopped using online banking because they're afraid.""",
203: """Internet usage is quite different among people of different age groups. Young people, like teenagers and people in their twenties, use the internet mainly for entertainment and social connection. They spend a lot of time on social media, like TikTok and Instagram, watching short videos, chatting with friends, and following celebrities. They also play online games and shop online very often. They're very quick to learn new apps and follow trends. People in their thirties and forties, like me, use the internet more for practical purposes, like work, online banking, and reading the news, although we also watch videos to relax. Older people, like my parents, use the internet more simply. They mainly use it to read the news, watch videos on YouTube, and make video calls with their children and grandchildren. They often need help from younger family members when they try a new app.""",
204: """Five years ago, people already used smartphones a lot, but in a slightly different way. Most people used their phones for calling, texting, social media, and taking photos. Some people used banking apps, but many still preferred to pay with cash, especially at small shops and markets. Video calls were not very common, and online shopping was mostly for young people. Today, there are some big changes. The biggest one is that phones have become our wallets. Now people pay for almost everything with QR codes, even at street food stalls. Another big change is that phones are now used for work. Many people have meetings and share documents on their phones. Also, short videos have become extremely popular, so people spend a lot more time watching them. Finally, during and after the pandemic, people started using their phones for things like online classes and ordering groceries.""",
205: """In my country, many people share this concern about young people and their phones. A lot of parents and teachers worry that kids spend too much time on their phones, especially on social media and games. They say that young people are losing face-to-face communication skills. For example, you can often see groups of teenagers sitting together in a cafe, but everyone is looking at their own phone instead of talking. Some people also worry that too much screen time affects children's sleep, eyesight, and studies. On the other hand, some people think phones are not all bad. Young people use them to learn new skills, stay in touch with friends, and even study English. Personally, I think the problem is how much and how phones are used. Parents should set clear rules, like no phones during meals, and encourage kids to do more outdoor activities.""",
206: """Shopping habits have changed a lot over the years. The most significant change is the rise of online shopping. In the past, people went to traditional markets or small shops to buy almost everything. Now, many people buy clothes, cosmetics, household items, and even groceries on shopping apps, because it's cheap and convenient, and the items are delivered to their door. Another change is the way people pay. People used to pay only with cash, but now card payments and QR codes are everywhere. Livestream shopping has also become very popular recently, where sellers show products live on social media and people buy them in real time. In addition, supermarkets and shopping malls have replaced many small shops in big cities, and people often go to malls not just to shop but also to eat and have fun. People also compare prices online much more before buying something.""",
207: """These days, I hear shoppers talking a lot about a few kinds of products. One of the most popular is smart home devices, like robot vacuum cleaners and air purifiers. People are interested in robot vacuums because they save time, and air purifiers have become popular because of air pollution in big cities, especially in winter. Another popular product is electric motorbikes and electric cars. Many people are interested in them because gas prices have gone up, and they're also better for the environment. A Vietnamese car company has also been promoting its electric cars a lot, so people talk about them. Skincare and health products are also very popular, especially among young people, who learn about them from social media reviews. I think these products are generating so much interest because people care more about convenience, health, and the environment than before.""",
208: """In my country, people go to many different places in their free time. In big cities, one of the most popular places is shopping malls. They're air-conditioned, which is great in the hot weather, and people can shop, eat, watch movies, and let their kids play there. Parks are also very popular, especially in the early morning and evening, when people go there to exercise, walk, or relax. Coffee shops are another favorite place. Vietnamese people love spending hours at cafes chatting with friends. On longer holidays, many people go to the beach, like Da Nang, Nha Trang, or Sam Son, or to mountain areas like Sa Pa and Da Lat to enjoy the cool weather. Recently, camping and glamping sites near cities have become popular too, especially among young people and families who want to escape the city for a weekend.""",
209: """In the past, when I was a university student, I had much more free time than now. My classes were only in the morning or afternoon, so I had a lot of time to hang out with my friends. We often went to cafes, played football, or rode our motorbikes around the city at night. On weekends, we sometimes took short trips together. My free time was very flexible, and I could do whatever I wanted. Now things are very different. I have a full-time job, a wife, and a five-year-old daughter, so I have much less free time. On weekdays, I'm usually tired after work, so I just relax at home with my family. On weekends, my free time is mostly spent with my daughter, like taking her to the park or visiting my parents. Even though I have less free time, I think I use it in a more meaningful way.""",
}
